<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cuiabá</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cuiabá is the capital of the Brazilian state of Mato Grosso, near the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. It was founded in 1719 after bandeirantes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> found gold in the area, and miners reached it by long river convoys. It replaced Vila Bela as the capital of Mato Grosso in 1835.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuiaba.jpg" alt="Cuiabá" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arne Müseler · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuiab%C3%A1_center.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cuiabá lies near the geographical centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>, in a transition zone between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a>, the Cerrado and the Pantanal, and is called the Southern gate to the Amazon. Its name is a Bororo word meaning 'arrow-fishing'. It is known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> as one of the hottest cities, with temperatures often above 40 °C. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> in the centre marks the middle of the continent as calculated in 1909; more accurate measurements in the 1990s placed it about 45 kilometres to the north-east, near Chapada dos <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guimaraes">Guimarães</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first Portuguese in the region were bandeirantes, explorers, slavers and fortune hunters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a>. In 1718 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pascoal-moreira-cabral">Pascoal Moreira Cabral</a> of Sorocaba arrived and, after losing a battle with the Coxiponé people, found gold up the Coxipó River. The bandeirantes enslaved indigenous people to work the site. Cabral reported the discovery in a letter of 8 April 1719, and a gold rush followed. An earthquake in 1746 destroyed much of the town. In 1751 Dom Antônio Rolim de Moura Tavares became first governor of the Captaincy of Mato Grosso and founded Vila Bela as its capital.</p><p>Cuiabá replaced Vila Bela as capital of the province in 1835. The town stayed small until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a> (1864 to 1870), which brought a brief boom as it supplied the troops, and afterwards governments used it as a place of exile for troublesome politicians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Roads and then aviation ended the isolation from 1930, and the city grew rapidly after 1960, from 50,000 inhabitants to more than a quarter of a million by 1980, as commercial agriculture carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soybean">soybeans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> abroad. Cuiabá is the capital and largest city of Mato Grosso and, with Várzea Grande, forms its metropolitan area. It is a trading centre for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-raising and farming region and the seat of the Federal University of Mato Grosso.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cuiabá” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Paulista bandeirantes led by Pascoal Moreira Cabral found gold near <b>Cuiabá</b> in 1718, and miners reached it by long canoe convoys called monções from São Paulo.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-mato-grosso">Captaincy of Mato Grosso</a></li></ul></section>
